local _, ns = ...
ns.ZoneLore = {
[1411]={articles={"Durotar"},text="Durotar, named after Thrall's father Durotan, lies on the eastern coast of Kalimdor, between the Barrens and Azshara. Its land is rocky, red and cracked, hot and dry, much like the orcs' lost homeland of Draenor, and dangerous creatures shelter in its crags and canyons.\n\nYoung orcs prove themselves in the Valley of Trials before facing the world beyond. Since the orcs founded Orgrimmar, Durotar has become a home for all the Horde's races, above all the Darkspear trolls of the Echo Isles."},
[1412]={articles={"Mulgore","Earthen Echo"},text="The green plains of Mulgore, home of the tauren, lie in the foothills of the Stonetalon Mountains, walled in by mountains on every side. Thunder Bluff towers over the northern plains, Bloodhoof Village stands at their heart, and young tauren train at Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. On the Skywatcher Plateau in the northwest, Muln Earthfury and the elders of the Earthen Ring council study the relics brought to them from Kalimdor's buried ruins.\n\nFor all its peace, Mulgore has troubles: quilboar overrun Brambleblade Ravine and the sacred Red Rocks, the Venture Company mines the eastern mountains, the Grimtotem poison the wells, Windfury harpies claim the far north, and Palemane gnolls camp in the south."},
[1413]={articles={"The Barrens"},text="The Barrens is a vast, dry savanna in the heart of Kalimdor, held mostly by the Horde, with low mountains for its borders. Around the Crossroads in the north lie a handful of oases, fed by the waters of the Wailing Caverns, which carry a life energy that invigorates every plant and beast that drinks them.\n\nLong ago the Barrens was forest, tended by the night elves. The Burning Legion, war and the Great Sundering turned it into the barren expanse it is now, though there is more to it than dry soil and sand.",chapters={{after=850,articles={"Kolkar Leaders (Classic)"},text="The Kolkar centaurs have plagued the tauren for years, but they were never more than disorganised raiders here, until new leaders rose to rally them. Barak Kodobane, who camped west of the Forgotten Pools, was only one strand of a tangled thread: other Kolkar leaders still gather their warbands across the Barrens."},{after=95508,articles={"Trouble in the Valley","Unwelcome Guests (Barrens)"},text="The hermit tanner on the ridge above the Dry Hills is Walton, once a sailor of the Kul Tiras navy. When Lord Admiral Proudmoore took Theramore and began his campaign in Durotar, Walton objected to its methods, above all the execution of civilians, and deserted. Kul Tiran marines have come to the Barrens hunting for him, and with a little help, he lived through their visit; but his past has caught up with him at last."},{after=97250,articles={"Wrongly Blamed, Justly Corrected"},text="Tension is rising along the eastern coast. Alliance soldiers have crossed into the Barrens at the border with Dustwallow Marsh, toppling the landmarks the tauren pathfinders left behind and blaming the Horde for a tavern burned to the ground. Camp Taurajo drove them back, though even its grunts doubt the Horde had a hand in the fire: too many of its own people rely on that road."}}},
[1420]={articles={"Tirisfal Glades","Bandarion Keep"},text="Tirisfal Glades, \"Tyr's Fall\", is an eerie, wooded region on the northern coast of Lordaeron, named for the Titan Keeper Tyr. It is the land of the Forsaken, soaked through with the Lich King's plague: its sky is a gloomy, blighted green, and its trees cling to their last shreds of life.\n\nThe Forsaken share it with old enemies: the Scarlet Crusade, whose Scarlet Monastery stands in the glades, and the Scourge, who haunt its derelict farms and mills. In the west, on the bluffs by the Whispering Forest, a new order of Forsaken paladins holds Bandarion Keep.",chapters={{after=96898,articles={"Remnants of War"},text="Not long ago, the necropolis Naxxramas bore down on the Undercity, meaning to wipe the Forsaken off the map. The Forsaken and the Argent Dawn drove it off, and both paid dearly for it. In the battle, the great necrotic crystal atop Naxxramas shattered, scattering its fragments across the glades, and the Cult of the Damned has come to gather them. Even spent, the shards are dangerous to touch."},{after=96899,articles={"Bandarion Keep (quest)","Bandarion Keep"},text="Bandarion Keep was once Dawn's Watch, where the Argent Dawn readied itself against the Scourge's last push into Tirisfal. When the Dawn withdrew to the Plaguelands it fell into disrepair, until Danitha Morr and her paladins took it over, restored it, and named it after a friend she shared with the Argent emissary Leonid Barthalomew."},{after=96896,articles={"A Righteous Cause","Bandarion Keep"},text="That friend was Garek Bandarion, perhaps the first Forsaken to wield the Light again, who fell defending the Undercity against Naxxramas. Leonid came on behalf of Lord Tyrosus to offer Danitha's order a place in the Argent Dawn, where its Light would be valued. She refused: the Forsaken are still the people her paladins swore to protect. Many Forsaken, remembering what the living did in the Light's name, mistrust paladins deeply, and that is why she means to stay."},{after=91317,articles={"The Tarnished"},text="Not every risen paladin has made peace with undeath. The Tarnished carried their old prejudices into it, and from the ruins of Shadowvale they turned their blades on their fellow Forsaken, each killing feeding the mistrust Danitha's order is trying to overcome. With their commander dead, the order's place in Tirisfal is a little surer, though Danitha expects it may take years, even decades, for the Forsaken to embrace them."}}},
[1426]={articles={"Dun Morogh"},text="Dun Morogh is a range of snowy, forested mountains in western Khaz Modan, the homeland of the dwarves and gnomes. Ironforge is carved into Ironforge Mountain, and Gnomeregan lies in the foothills of Chill Breeze Valley.\n\nIts peaks are the highest in the Eastern Kingdoms: their glaciers feed the rivers of Khaz Modan, and their rock gives the dwarves all the ore they could want. Mountains wall it in on every side, and dwarven troops watch the few passes in."},
[1429]={articles={"Elwynn Forest"},text="Elwynn Forest is a vast woodland of stately hardwoods, the heartland of the human kingdom of Stormwind and where every human adventurer begins. Stormwind City lies to the northwest, the Redridge Mountains to the east, and Duskwood to the south, across the river.\n\nMuch of the forest burned during the First War, but its trees and glades have grown back. Marshal McBride keeps the peace in Northshire Valley, and Marshal Dughan in Goldshire."},
[1438]={articles={"Teldrassil"},text="Teldrassil, \"Crown of the Earth\", is the World Tree the night elves grew on an island off Kalimdor's northwestern coast after the Third War. Its crown holds their capital, Darnassus, and its boughs hold Rut'theran Village, Dolanaar and Shadowglen, amid twilit forests with a world of their own.\n\nUnlike Nordrassil before it, Teldrassil was never blessed by the Dragon Aspects, which leaves it open to corruption. It is the Alliance's greatest stronghold in Kalimdor, and young night elves set out into the world from its shelter."},
[1442]={articles={"Stonetalon Mountains"},text="The Stonetalon Mountains rise west of the Barrens, between Ashenvale and Desolace. They were the harpies' ancestral home, though turmoil has thinned their numbers, and Stonetalon Peak, the tallest of them, has long been sacred to both the night elves and the tauren.\n\nThe tauren hold Sun Rock Retreat at the heart of the range, and the Darkspear trolls a village at Malaka'jin in the south. Windshear Crag has been ravaged by years of logging, its air thick with soot and its water fouled with oil, and beyond it the Charred Vale runs down to the sea, home to black dragons and elementals.",chapters={{after=1062,articles={"Goblin Invaders"},text="The goblin-run Venture Company has moved into Stonetalon, felling its trees and burning great stretches of forest, and the spirits of the mountains are nearly blind with pain and rage. The Horde has struck back at the loggers in Windshear Crag, but it may take the wisdom of the tauren elders to calm the spirits."},{after=6301,articles={"Cycle of Rebirth"},text="At Sun Rock Retreat, Tammra Windfield is working to heal what industrial greed and endless war have done to Stonetalon. She gathers Gaea seeds from the shores of Mirkfallon Lake and imbues them with the Earthmother's blessing, so that new life can take root even in the Charred Vale."}}},
[2521]={articles={"Zephras Isle","Skyborne"},text="Zephras Isle is a flying island in Skywall, the Elemental Plane of Air, and the home of the Skyborne. The air elementals who once lived there beside them vanished years ago, and since then the magic holding the island up has been fading.\n\nThe Skyborne's other islands, Anvilas and Siroccas, have fallen silent and are collapsing, and their refugees are fleeing to Zephras. But Zephras is starting to fall apart too.\n\nThe Skyborne, who call themselves the shen'dorei, descend from Highborne who left Eldre'Thalas some ten thousand years ago, and lived beside the wind spirits ever since. An elected Council of Elders governs them, led on Zephras by High Elder Talaanis Shadowsong. Two orders guide their people: the Windshapers, shaman who honour the elements, and the High Order, heirs of the Highborne magisters, who seek the arcane lore of their forebears.",chapters={{after=92465,articles={"Agitators","Return to Rorian"},text="Not every shen'dorei is willing to wait for the spirits to return. The Al'Aketh cult has been stirring the simple wind sprites by Thendal's standing stones into violence, and shen'dorei blood has now been shed in the grove. Rorian the Dayseeker had long watched the cult from afar; this, he says, is a clear escalation."},{after=92472,articles={"The Next Step (Skyborne)","Skyborne"},text="The Al'Aketh are zealots devoted to Al'Akir the Windlord, the elemental ruler of Skywall, a name the wind spirits rarely spoke. Their leaders are what remains of an older order of his priests, grown strong on the fear the spirits' disappearance left behind, and some of the refugees from the failing islands have joined them just to survive. Brutal and single-minded, they now threaten Shen'dar Village to the south."},{after=93746,articles={"A Firm Response"},text="The crisis has strained the bond between the Skyborne's two orders. The High Order has been warned to keep out of the Windshapers' affairs, and when Belathaan Brightwish ignored the warning, the Windshapers met him on the road to the Shrine of Akir to send him home with a few bruises and a healthy respect for their boundaries."},{after=93090,articles={"What Comes Next (Horde)"},text="With the Al'Aketh defeated, the Windshapers have found new friends in the Earthen Ring and, through them, joined the Horde. With High Elder Shadowsong's blessing, their shaman are leaving Zephras to seek a lasting answer to the island's failing magic. Muln Earthfury has helped open a skycutter route to Mulgore, where the Earthen Ring make their home, and some of the Windshapers are already waiting on the other side."}}},
}
